#
# Touch scrolling resampled to the display's frames.
#
# A drag's moves reach the scrolling thread at the touchscreen's rate (about
# 110-125 Hz) against the panel's 90 Hz, and each scrolled the page as it
# came: at a steady finger a frame moved one move's distance, two, or none
# (5.6, 11.2 or 0 CSS px on the Jolla Phone); 28% of the frames composited
# mid-drag did not move. Chrome and Android resample touch input to the
# frame instead.
#
# Once a drag's first move has scrolled on the scrolling thread (not when
# the page needs main-thread scrolling), its later moves are queued, and each
# display refresh (patch 0007) applies the finger's movement up to 16 ms
# before the frame, interpolated within a move. Moves are timed by the touch
# reports' own times, mapped to now with the smallest delay seen: the UI
# process sends one wheel event at a time and merges those that wait, so they
# arrive in bursts. The delay behind the frame covers that path (6 and 12 ms
# left 6-9% of frames still, 16 ms 4-7%; PACIFIC_SCROLL_RESAMPLE_LATENCY_MS).
# Anything that is not a drag move first flushes the queue, report by report
# at their own times, so the end of a drag and its fling keep their measured
# velocity. The page still gets its DOM wheel events as they come.
#
# Dragging at a steady 125 Hz finger on the Jolla Phone, frames that did not
# move: OSNews 0.9% from 28%, The Verge 4.7% from 28%. Flings travel about 5%
# further: queued moves are acknowledged at once, so fewer reach the fling's
# velocity estimate merged. PACIFIC_SCROLL_RESAMPLE=0 turns it off.
#
